module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8692', KITS: {"3.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.1.1","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Theme 1 covers identity and relationships, healthy living, and education and work — the three areas of your own life you are most often asked to describe, compare and give opinions on. Talking about people needs adjectives that agree and comparisons; talking about habits needs frequency phrases and desde hace; talking about school and the future needs impersonal rules and si + present + future. Getting these structures secure lets you say what you actually think, not just recite set phrases.","idea":[{"code":"Topic 1","h":"Identity and relationships with others","t":"This topic covers describing yourself and the people close to you: physical description, personality, and family and friendship relationships. Build description from three layers — who someone is, what they are like, and why the relationship matters to you: Mi mejor amiga se llama Laura; es muy graciosa y siempre me hace reír (My best friend is called Laura; she is very funny and always makes me laugh). Personality adjectives agree like any other adjective: gracioso/graciosa, simpático/simpática, trabajador/trabajadora. Comparisons are common in this topic: Mi hermano es más alto que yo, pero yo soy más paciente que él (My brother is taller than me, but I am more patient than him). The specification notes that you are expected to respond according to your own interests and experiences, so prepare true, specific detail about your own family and friends rather than generic sentences."},{"code":"Topic 2","h":"Healthy living and lifestyle","t":"This topic covers diet, exercise, sleep and habits, both good and bad, and giving opinions with reasons about them. State a habit, then evaluate it: Como muchas verduras y hago deporte tres veces por semana, así que llevo una vida bastante sana (I eat a lot of vegetables and do sport three times a week, so I lead quite a healthy life). Negative habits use the same structures: Algunos jóvenes fuman o comen demasiada comida basura, lo cual es malo para la salud (Some young people smoke or eat too much junk food, which is bad for their health). desde hace + a time period describes something continuing from the past into the present: Hago yoga desde hace dos años (I have been doing yoga for two years). Reflexive verbs are common here too: acostarse, despertarse, cuidarse (to go to bed, to wake up, to look after oneself). You are expected to give a genuine opinion with a reason whenever this topic comes up, not just describe a habit."},{"code":"Topic 3","h":"Education and work","t":"This topic covers school subjects and opinions of them, rules and pressures at school, and part-time jobs or future career plans. Give a subject opinion with a reason: Prefiero las ciencias porque el profesor explica todo muy claramente (I prefer science because the teacher explains everything very clearly). School rules use hay que or se debe: Hay que llevar uniforme y no se puede usar el móvil en clase (You have to wear uniform and you cannot use your phone in class). Future plans typically combine the simple future or ir a + infinitive with a conditional structure built on si: Si apruebo mis exámenes, estudiaré en la universidad; si no, buscaré un trabajo a tiempo parcial (If I pass my exams, I will study at university; if not, I will look for a part-time job) — si with the present tense pairs with the future tense in the result clause, not the conditional. You are expected to give a genuine opinion with a reason about school and work, drawing on your own experience rather than a generic answer."}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a friend using comparison","setup":"Translate into Spanish (the speaker is male): 'My friend Marta is more sociable than me, but I am funnier than her.'","steps":["'More sociable than me' uses más + adjective + que: más sociable que yo.","sociable does not change for gender, so it stays the same form for Marta.","'Funnier than her' follows the same pattern: más gracioso + que ella — gracioso agrees with the male speaker.","Full sentence: Mi amiga Marta es más sociable que yo, pero yo soy más gracioso que ella."],"cues":["Which comparison structure means 'more … than'?","Does sociable change its ending for a feminine subject?","Which pronoun follows que when comparing to 'her'?","Write the complete sentence with both comparisons."]},{"title":"Translating a healthy-living text","setup":"Translate into English: 'Como mucha fruta y verdura, y también hago ejercicio cada mañana antes de ir al colegio. Sin embargo, no duermo lo suficiente entre semana porque tengo muchos deberes.'","steps":["The first sentence has two linked habits: como mucha fruta y verdura (I eat a lot of fruit and vegetables) and hago ejercicio cada mañana antes de ir al colegio (I do exercise every morning before going to school).","sin embargo signals a contrast: 'however'.","no duermo lo suficiente entre semana translates as 'I don't sleep enough during the week'.","Full English answer: I eat a lot of fruit and vegetables, and I also exercise every morning before going to school. However, I don't sleep enough during the week because I have a lot of homework."],"cues":["What two healthy habits are named in the first sentence?","What does sin embargo signal is coming next?","What is the writer's problem with sleep?","Give the complete English translation of both sentences."]},{"title":"School rules with hay que and se puede","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'At my school you have to arrive on time, and you cannot eat in class.'","steps":["'You have to' is the impersonal hay que + infinitive: hay que llegar.","'on time' is the set phrase a la hora, not a word-for-word 'en tiempo': hay que llegar a la hora.","'You cannot' is no se puede + infinitive: no se puede comer en clase.","Full sentence, with the place phrase first: En mi colegio hay que llegar a la hora, y no se puede comer en clase."],"cues":["Which impersonal phrase means 'you have to', and what verb form follows it?","Is 'on time' translated word for word, or with a set phrase?","Which impersonal phrase means 'you cannot'?","Where does 'at my school' go, and how are the two halves joined?"]},{"title":"Future plans with si + present + future","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'If I pass my exams, I will study medicine; if not, I will look for a job.'","steps":["'If I pass' uses si with the present tense: si apruebo mis exámenes.","The result clause takes the simple future, not the conditional: estudiaré medicina.","'If not' is si no, and its result also takes the simple future: buscaré un trabajo.","Full sentence: Si apruebo mis exámenes, estudiaré medicina; si no, buscaré un trabajo."],"cues":["Which tense follows si here — present, or future?","Which tense goes in the result clause after that si clause?","How do you say 'if not', and what tense follows it?","Write the complete sentence, both halves joined correctly."]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'more … than' and 'less … than'.","a":"más … que; menos … que."},{"q":"What does desde hace + a time period express?","a":"Something that has been continuing from the past up to now — 'I have been doing X for [time period]'."},{"q":"Which tense goes in the result clause after si + present tense ('if I do X, I will do Y')?","a":"The simple future, not the conditional."},{"q":"Give the Spanish impersonal phrase for 'you cannot' plus an infinitive.","a":"No se puede + infinitive."}]},"room":{"facts":["Personality and description adjectives agree with the person described: gracioso/graciosa, trabajador/trabajadora, simpático/simpática.","Comparison: más/menos + adjective + que (more/less … than); tan + adjective + como (as … as).","desde hace + time period = 'have been doing X for [time]', with the verb in the present tense.","School rules: hay que / se debe + infinitive (you have to); no se puede + infinitive (you cannot).","si + present tense pairs with the future tense in the result clause: Si apruebo, estudiaré (If I pass, I will study) — never si + present with a conditional result."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into English: 'Mi hermana es más trabajadora que yo.'","a":"My sister is harder-working / more hard-working than me.","sol":"My sister is harder-working than me. más trabajadora que yo is the comparative 'more … than' structure, with trabajadora agreeing with hermana (feminine).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["más ... que is a comparison — which way does it point, more or less?","The adjective is built from a verb you know — what personal quality does that give?","Give the complete English sentence, keeping the comparative meaning."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I eat a lot of vegetables every day.'","a":"Como muchas verduras todos los días.","sol":"Como muchas verduras todos los días. como is the yo form of comer; muchas agrees with verduras (feminine plural).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["What is the yo form of comer?","Check the gender and number of the noun before choosing the ending of the quantity word.","Verb, then the quantity word agreeing with its noun, then the time phrase."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'You have to wear uniform at my school.'","a":"Hay que llevar uniforme en mi colegio.","sol":"Hay que llevar uniforme en mi colegio. hay que + infinitive is the impersonal 'you have to'; the phrase does not change for who is being addressed.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase means 'you have to'?","What form does the verb after hay que take?","Impersonal phrase, infinitive, object, then the place."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Give the Spanish for: 'I have been learning Spanish for three years.'","a":"Aprendo español desde hace tres años.","sol":"Aprendo español desde hace tres años. desde hace + a time period, with the verb in the present tense (aprendo), expresses an action continuing up to now.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which tense does the verb take with desde hace, present or past?","Where does desde hace go in the sentence?","Present-tense verb, object, then the time expression at the end."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Mis padres son muy estrictos: no me dejan salir entre semana y tengo que hacer mis deberes antes de cenar. Sin embargo, los fines de semana soy bastante libre.' (a) Give one thing the writer's parents do not allow. (b) What is different at the weekend?","a":"(a) They don't let the writer go out during the week. (b) The writer is fairly free (has more freedom) at the weekend.","sol":"(a) No me dejan salir entre semana — they don't let me go out during the week. (b) los fines de semana soy bastante libre — at the weekends I am fairly free. sin embargo (however) signals the contrast between the strict weekday rules and the freer weekend.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the sentence before sin embargo for what is not allowed.","sin embargo signals a change — read what follows it for the second answer.","Two short English answers: (a) one thing that is forbidden, (b) how the weekend differs."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Although I don't like exercise, I know it's important for my health.'","a":"Aunque no me gusta el ejercicio, sé que es importante para mi salud.","sol":"Aunque no me gusta el ejercicio, sé que es importante para mi salud. aunque (although) introduces the contrast; sé is the irregular yo form of saber, followed by que and a clause.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which word means 'although'?","Which verb means 'to know [a fact]', and is it regular in the yo form?","Write the complete sentence, keeping both clauses linked by aunque."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'In my opinion, homework is useful, but sometimes we have too much.'","a":"En mi opinión, los deberes son útiles, pero a veces tenemos demasiados.","sol":"En mi opinión, los deberes son útiles, pero a veces tenemos demasiados. deberes is plural, so son and útiles both agree; demasiados agrees with deberes (masculine plural) even though the noun itself is not repeated.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Is deberes singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb and adjective?","'too much' here stands in for the noun just mentioned — make the quantity word agree with that noun.","Opinion phrase, subject + verb + adjective, then the contrast word, then verb + quantity word."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'El año que viene, si consigo buenas notas, iré a la universidad para estudiar medicina. Si no, buscaré un aprendizaje en un hospital, porque siempre me ha interesado ayudar a la gente.' Explain both of the writer's possible plans and the reason given for the second one.","a":"If they get good grades, they will go to university to study medicine; if not, they will look for an apprenticeship in a hospital, because they have always been interested in helping people.","sol":"El año que viene, si consigo buenas notas, iré a la universidad para estudiar medicina — next year, if I get good grades, I will go to university to study medicine. Si no, buscaré un aprendizaje en un hospital — if not, I will look for an apprenticeship in a hospital, porque siempre me ha interesado ayudar a la gente — because I have always been interested in helping people.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The text gives two different possible futures, joined by si and si no.","Find the reason clause at the very end, introduced by porque.","Give both plans and the reason, all in English."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My grandmother is as generous as she is patient, and she has looked after me since I was a child.'","a":"Mi abuela es tan generosa como paciente, y me ha cuidado desde que yo era pequeño/a.","sol":"Mi abuela es tan generosa como paciente, y me ha cuidado desde que yo era pequeño/a. tan…como makes the equal comparison between two adjectives describing the same person; me ha cuidado is the present perfect of cuidar with me as a direct object pronoun ('has looked after me'); desde que + a past tense clause (era) gives 'since [a point in the past]', different from desde hace + a time period. Adding yo before era makes clear that it is the speaker, not the grandmother, who was a child.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which comparison structure joins two adjectives about the same person as equally true?","What tense is 'has looked after', and which pronoun does it need?","One sentence: the comparison clause, then y, then the perfect-tense clause ending with a 'since' clause that has its own verb and subject."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish about your lifestyle and future plans, covering: (1) a healthy or unhealthy habit and your opinion of it, (2) a school rule you have to follow, (3) what you will do if you pass your exams and what you will do if you do not.","a":"A paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish, covering all three bullet points with an opinion, a school rule using hay que or se puede, and a si + present + future structure for both possible outcomes.","sol":"Llevo una vida bastante sana porque hago deporte tres veces por semana y como mucha fruta, aunque a veces como demasiada comida basura los fines de semana. En mi colegio hay que llevar uniforme y no se puede usar el móvil en clase; a veces me parece estricto, pero entiendo las reglas. Si apruebo mis exámenes este verano, estudiaré ciencias y luego iré a la universidad. Si no consigo las notas que necesito, buscaré un aprendizaje en la sanidad, porque siempre me ha interesado ayudar a la gente. (88 words: habit + opinion, a school rule, and both si + present + future outcomes.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which structures from this room express a habit, an obligation and a condition?","The school-rule bullet needs an impersonal structure.","The future bullet needs two linked clauses, one for each possible outcome."],"codes":["Topic 1","Topic 2","Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Even though my brother and I argue sometimes, we get on well in general, and I know I can always count on him.'","a":"Aunque mi hermano y yo discutimos a veces, nos llevamos bien en general, y sé que siempre puedo contar con él.","sol":"Aunque mi hermano y yo discutimos a veces, nos llevamos bien en general, y sé que siempre puedo contar con él. nos llevamos bien is the reflexive-style expression for 'we get on well'; contar con is 'to count/rely on', taking con before the pronoun él.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which reflexive expression means 'to get on well' with someone?","'Count on' needs a specific preposition before the pronoun — which one?","Write the complete sentence with all three clauses."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I try to sleep enough during the week, but sometimes I go to bed very late because I have too much homework.'","a":"Intento dormir lo suficiente entre semana, pero a veces me acuesto muy tarde porque tengo demasiados deberes.","sol":"Intento dormir lo suficiente entre semana, pero a veces me acuesto muy tarde porque tengo demasiados deberes. intentar + infinitive gives 'to try to'; me acuesto is the reflexive acostarse (o→ue), 'I go to bed'; demasiados agrees with deberes (masculine plural).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which verb means 'to try to' + another verb?","Which reflexive verb means 'to go to bed', and does its stem change?","One complete sentence with two linked clauses, joined by pero."],"codes":["Topic 2","Topic 3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'as … as' (equal comparison).","a":"tan … como"},{"q":"Which tense goes in the result clause after si + present tense?","a":"The simple future."},{"q":"Translate: 'no se puede usar el móvil en clase'.","a":"You cannot use your phone in class."},{"q":"Give the Spanish reflexive expression for 'to get on well' (with someone).","a":"llevarse bien (con alguien)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"gracioso/a, trabajador(a), simpático/a","back":"funny, hard-working, nice/kind (agree with the person). Mi amigo es muy gracioso. (My friend is very funny.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"más/menos ... que","back":"more/less ... than. Es más alto que yo. (He is taller than me.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"llevarse bien / mal (con alguien)","back":"to get on well / badly (with someone). Me llevo bien con mi hermana. (I get on well with my sister.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"contar con alguien","back":"to count/rely on someone. Puedo contar con mis amigos. (I can count on my friends.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"llevar una vida sana","back":"to lead a healthy life. Llevo una vida bastante sana. (I lead quite a healthy life.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"hacer deporte / hacer ejercicio","back":"to do sport / to exercise. Hago deporte tres veces por semana. (I do sport three times a week.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"desde hace + tiempo","back":"for [time period] (continuing to now). Hago yoga desde hace dos años. (I have been doing yoga for two years.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"la comida basura","back":"junk food. Como demasiada comida basura. (I eat too much junk food.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"hay que / se debe + infinitivo","back":"you have to. Hay que llegar a la hora. (You have to arrive on time.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"no se puede + infinitivo","back":"you cannot. No se puede comer en clase. (You cannot eat in class.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"un aprendizaje","back":"an apprenticeship. Buscaré un aprendizaje. (I will look for an apprenticeship.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"si + presente..., + futuro","back":"if + present..., + future (not conditional). Si apruebo, estudiaré medicina. (If I pass, I will study medicine.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: identidad y relaciones","items":["gracioso/a - funny","trabajador(a) - hard-working","simpático/a - nice, kind","generoso/a - generous","paciente - patient","llevarse bien / mal (con) - to get on well / badly (with)","contar con alguien - to count on someone","el mejor amigo / la mejor amiga - best friend","discutir - to argue","cuidar (a alguien) - to look after (someone)"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: vida sana","items":["llevar una vida sana - to lead a healthy life","hacer deporte / ejercicio - to do sport / exercise","la comida basura - junk food","las verduras / la fruta - vegetables / fruit","dormir lo suficiente - to sleep enough","acostarse / despertarse - to go to bed / to wake up","cuidarse - to look after oneself"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: educación y trabajo","items":["el uniforme - uniform","los deberes - homework","un aprendizaje - an apprenticeship","aprobar (un examen) - to pass (an exam)","la sanidad - healthcare","estricto/a - strict","las ciencias - science","a tiempo parcial - part-time"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: comparaciones, hay que y el futuro con si","items":["más/menos + adjetivo + que: Es más alto que yo. (He is taller than me.)","tan + adjetivo + como: Es tan paciente como generosa. (She is as patient as she is generous.)","desde hace + tiempo (presente): Vivo aquí desde hace cinco años. (I have lived here for five years.)","desde que + cláusula (pasado): Me cuida desde que era niño. (She has looked after me since I was a child.)","hay que / se debe + infinitivo: Hay que estudiar más. (You have to study more.)","no se puede + infinitivo: No se puede fumar aquí. (You cannot smoke here.)","si + presente, + futuro: Si trabajo mucho, aprobaré. (If I work hard, I will pass.)","el futuro simple, formado sobre el infinitivo (ser e ir incluidos): estudiaré, buscaré, iré, seré. Si apruebo, iré a la universidad. (If I pass, I'll go to university.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:15:11.702Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key re-solved correctly and the Spanish throughout is accurate, so this is close to publishable; fix the missing second vocabulary field per topic, the misleading 'a la hora' claim in worked example 3, and add accepted alternatives to the desde hace question before release."}}},"3.2.2.1":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.2.2.1","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Higher tier noun phrases add two things to what Foundation already covers: the demonstrative aquel (and its neuter and pronoun forms) for pointing at something further away, and a set of pronoun forms — object pronoun word order, possessive pronouns, relative pronouns and pronouns after prepositions — that Paper 4's translation and writing tasks reward when they appear correctly, and that Papers 1 and 3 test you can recognise when reading or listening. Marks are lost less often for not knowing a rule exists than for using el que where a subject relative clause is needed, or writing el mío when the sentence actually needs mío as an adjective, so this room drills the exact distinctions the specification names.","idea":[{"code":"Determiners","h":"aquel: the third 'that/those', further away","t":"Spanish has three distances: este (this, near me), ese (that, near you), and aquel (that, over there — away from both of us). Aquel agrees like an -o/-a adjective: aquel chico, aquella chica, aquellos chicos, aquellas chicas. It sits before the noun: Aquellas montañas al fondo son muy altas (Those mountains in the background are very tall). You are allowed to use ese instead of aquel in your own writing and speaking without losing marks, but you must still recognise aquel, aquella, aquellos and aquellas when you meet them in a reading or listening text, and know that they mean something further away than ese."},{"code":"Pronouns","h":"Object pronoun order, possessives, relatives and pronouns after a preposition","t":"Object pronouns nos (us) and os (you, plural informal) work like all the others: before a conjugated verb, or attached to an infinitive — Os veo (I see you) / Os puedo ver or Puedo veros (I can see you); with two verbs, either position is correct. The same pronouns are also plural reflexives: Nos levantamos a las siete (We get up at seven); Os laváis las manos (You wash your hands); when the reflexive shows something done to each other rather than to yourself, it is reciprocal: Nos vemos todos los días (We see each other every day) — this reciprocal reading only makes sense for a plural subject. Possessive pronouns replace a noun already understood: el mío, la mía, el tuyo, el suyo, el nuestro, el vuestro (mine, yours, his/hers/theirs, ours, yours-plural), agreeing with the thing owned, not the owner — Mi coche es azul; el tuyo es rojo (My car is blue; yours is red). Keep this apart from the possessive adjective, which sits with a noun: mi coche (my car) uses mi as an adjective, whereas el mío means 'mine' on its own with no noun following. lo que (what, that which) never changes; it introduces what you don't yet know: No entiendo lo que dice (I don't understand what he's saying). el que, la que, los que, las que and el cual, la cual, los cuales, las cuales introduce a subject relative clause — the noun they stand for is the subject of the verb that follows. Only the el que set can stand on its own with no noun before it, meaning 'the one(s) who / whoever': El que estudia más saca mejores notas (The one who studies most gets the best marks). Both sets can pick out one of several people already mentioned, and here el cual is the more formal choice: Mi amiga, la que (or la cual) se sienta a mi lado, me ayudó (My friend, the one who sits next to me, helped me) — el cual always needs a stated noun in front of it. With a simple stated noun, que alone is the usual choice: El libro que leí ayer era interesante (The book that I read yesterday was interesting). The neuter pronoun aquello refers to a whole idea rather than one noun: Aquello no me gustó nada (I didn't like that at all). Other relative words keep their normal meaning: cuando (when), donde (where), que (that/which/who). After a preposition, the pronouns mí, ti, él, ella, usted, nosotros, vosotros, ellos/ellas replace a noun — para mí, con ella — except that con + mí and con + ti fuse into conmigo and contigo (¿Vienes conmigo? — Are you coming with me?). Adding a mí, a ti and so on after the verb is emphatic, not the object itself: A mí me gusta el fútbol (I really like football, emphasising 'I')."}],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing esta or aquella from a described scene","setup":"You are describing a photo. There are two mountains: one is right in the foreground, close to the church; the other is a small shape right at the back of the picture. Complete: '___ montaña, cerca de la iglesia, es más verde que ___ montaña al fondo.'","steps":["The first mountain is right in the foreground, the part of the photo closest to the describer, so it takes the nearest distance: Esta montaña.","The second mountain is described as being right at the back — the furthest point in the picture — so it takes the furthest distance: aquella montaña.","Check agreement: montaña is feminine singular, so both determiners must be feminine singular too: esta, aquella.","Complete sentence: Esta montaña, cerca de la iglesia, es más verde que aquella montaña al fondo."],"cues":["Where in the picture is each mountain, according to the description?","Which mountain is described as furthest away, and which determiner marks that distance?","What gender and number is montaña, and what does that do to both determiners?","What do you get when you put both determiners into the full sentence?"]},{"title":"Object pronoun position with a modal verb","setup":"Translate this exchange into Spanish, giving both correct word orders for the second line: 'Can you help us?' 'Yes, I can help you (plural).'","steps":["The first line has one conjugated verb (puedes) and one infinitive (ayudar); the object is nos, so it goes either before puedes or attached to ayudar: ¿Nos puedes ayudar? or ¿Puedes ayudarnos?","The reply also has a modal plus infinitive (puedo ayudar), and the object this time is os, giving the same two options.","Attach os to the infinitive: Sí, puedo ayudaros.","Or place it before the conjugated verb instead, giving the second correct answer: Sí, os puedo ayudar."],"cues":["How many verbs are in 'can you help us', and which one is conjugated?","In a two-verb construction, where can the object pronoun go?","Attach os to ayudar first: what does that give you?","Now move the same pronoun in front of the conjugated verb instead: what changes?"]},{"title":"Headless el que versus a possessive pronoun","setup":"Translate: 'The one who arrives first has a car; mine is older than his.'","steps":["'The one who arrives first' names no specific noun before it, so it needs the headless subject relative: El que llega primero.","'has a car' completes that first clause: tiene un coche.","'Mine' stands in for coche with no noun following, so it needs the possessive pronoun, not the adjective: el mío.","'His' also stands alone for coche, agreeing with coche (masculine), not with the owner: el suyo. Full sentence: El que llega primero tiene un coche; el mío es más viejo que el suyo."],"cues":["Is there a specific noun named before 'who arrives first', or does the sentence start straight with it?","What completes that first clause?","Does 'mine' have a noun straight after it in the English, or does it stand on its own?","What does 'his' agree with here: the owner, or the thing owned?"]},{"title":"Reciprocal reflexive with nos","setup":"Translate: 'My best friend and I write to each other every week.' (Use escribirse.)","steps":["The subject is plural (mi mejor amigo y yo = nosotros), so the reflexive pronoun must be the plural form, nos.","Conjugate escribir for nosotros: escribimos.","Because the two people write to each other, not to themselves, nos here means 'each other' (reciprocal), so no extra word for 'each other' is needed: nos escribimos.","Add the time phrase: Mi mejor amigo y yo nos escribimos todas las semanas."],"cues":["Is the subject of this sentence singular or plural?","What is the nosotros form of escribir?","With a plural subject, can nos mean 'each other' as well as 'ourselves'?","Put the pronoun, verb and time phrase together in the right order."]}],"check":[{"q":"When can you use ese instead of aquel without losing marks?","a":"In your own writing and speaking (production) — but you must still recognise aquel/aquella/aquellos/aquellas and know they mean 'further away' when reading or listening."},{"q":"What is the difference between mi coche and el mío?","a":"mi coche uses the possessive adjective mi with the noun coche stated; el mío is the possessive pronoun standing in for coche, with no noun following."},{"q":"In 'Mi amiga, la que se sienta a mi lado, me ayudó', what does la que do?","a":"It picks out which friend is meant — 'the one who sits next to me' — as the subject of se sienta."},{"q":"What does conmigo mean, and how is it formed?","a":"'With me' — it is the fused form of con + mí, used instead of con mí."}]},"room":{"facts":["Three distances: este/esta (this), ese/esa (that, near you), aquel/aquella (that, further away) — aquellos/aquellas in the plural.","el mío/la mía/el tuyo/el suyo/el nuestro/el vuestro are possessive pronouns (stand alone, no noun follows); mi/tu/su etc with a noun are possessive adjectives (mi coche).","el que/la que/los que/las que introduce a subject relative clause and can stand alone, meaning 'the one(s) who/that' (El que estudia más saca mejores notas); el cual/la cual/los cuales/las cuales do the same job only after a stated noun (mi amiga, la cual se sienta a mi lado) and never stand alone; with a simple stated noun, que alone is the usual choice.","lo que never changes for gender or number; aquello is the neuter pronoun for a whole idea, not one noun.","con + mí = conmigo; con + ti = contigo. Other prepositional pronouns (para mí, con ella, a nosotros) keep their normal form.","nos and os, with a plural subject, can mean 'each other' (reciprocal) as well as 'ourselves/yourselves'."],"questions":[{"q":"Complete with the correct form of aquel: '___ edificio al fondo de la calle es el ayuntamiento.' (edificio = building)","a":"Aquel","sol":"Aquel edificio al fondo de la calle es el ayuntamiento. Edificio is masculine singular, and 'at the end of the street' signals the furthest distance, so aquel (not ese) is needed.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Decide the gender and number of edificio before choosing a form.","Where in the street is this building — close to the speaker, or far away?","Give one word only."],"codes":["Determiners"]},{"q":"Complete with the correct form of aquel: 'Aquellos zapatos son más baratos que ___ botas.' (zapatos = shoes, m; botas = boots, f)","a":"aquellas","sol":"Aquellos zapatos son más baratos que aquellas botas. Botas is feminine plural, so the second determiner must match it: aquellas, not the masculine aquellos already used for zapatos.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The first determiner is already given — look at what it agrees with.","Check the gender and number of botas, not zapatos.","Give one word only."],"codes":["Determiners"]},{"q":"Choose the correct word for the gap: '___ estudia más saca mejores notas.' (a) El que (b) Lo que (c) Aquello","a":"El que","sol":"El que estudia más saca mejores notas. El que stands for 'the one who / whoever' — a person, a specific kind of referent — so el que is correct; lo que would only fit for an abstract idea, not a person.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Is the gap standing in for a person, or for an abstract idea?","Does the subject of estudia have a specific kind of referent — a person — or none at all?","Write out your choice in full rather than giving a letter."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'The girl who lives next door is my friend.'","a":"La chica que vive al lado es mi amiga.","sol":"La chica que vive al lado es mi amiga. 'Who lives next door' describes chica, the subject of vive, so a subject relative is needed; que alone is what the specification tests for a relative clause attached to a stated noun like this.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the noun that 'who lives next door' is describing.","Is that noun the subject of the verb that follows it?","One clause, then mi amiga at the end."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My sister's bag is red; mine is black.'","a":"El bolso de mi hermana es rojo; el mío es negro.","sol":"El bolso de mi hermana es rojo; el mío es negro. 'Mine' stands alone for bolso (masculine) with no noun following it, so the possessive pronoun el mío is needed, not the adjective mío on its own.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Decide whether 'mine' has a noun straight after it in English.","The possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned, not with the speaker.","Two clauses joined by a semicolon."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Rewrite, moving the object pronoun to the other correct position: 'Quiero veros mañana.'","a":"Os quiero ver mañana.","sol":"Os quiero ver mañana. With a conjugated verb (quiero) plus an infinitive (ver), the plural object pronoun os may attach to the infinitive (veros) or move in front of the conjugated verb (os quiero); both are correct, and the task asks for the other one.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Find the two verbs in the sentence: which is conjugated and which is the infinitive?","The pronoun can go in front of the conjugated verb instead of attached to the infinitive.","Move only the pronoun; keep mañana at the end."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using a reciprocal reflexive: 'My cousins see each other every summer.'","a":"Mis primos se ven todos los veranos.","sol":"Mis primos se ven todos los veranos. The subject (mis primos) is plural, so the third-person plural reflexive se is used with the reciprocal meaning 'each other'; ver conjugates regularly to ven for ellos.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Which reflexive pronoun goes with a third-person plural subject?","With a plural subject, this reflexive can mean 'each other'.","Verb, then the time phrase, in that order."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'No entendí lo que dijo el profesor, pero mi amiga, la que se sienta a mi lado, me lo explicó.'","a":"I didn't understand what the teacher said, but my friend, the one who sits next to me, explained it to me.","sol":"No entendí lo que dijo el profesor, pero mi amiga, la que se sienta a mi lado, me lo explicó. lo que stands for the whole idea of what the teacher said; la que here picks out a specific friend already named, as the subject of se sienta.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["lo que refers to a whole idea, not one noun — what does the teacher's speech mean here?","la que picks out a specific person already named — who?","Give one complete English sentence."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Are you coming with me? No, I'm going with her.'","a":"¿Vienes conmigo? No, voy con ella.","sol":"¿Vienes conmigo? No, voy con ella. Con + mí fuses to conmigo; ella is unaffected by con and keeps its normal prepositional pronoun form.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["con + mí does not stay as two words — what does it become?","Does con fuse with every pronoun, or only mí and ti?","Two short sentences, the second starting with No."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, choosing the correct possessive forms: 'My phone is old; yours is new.'","a":"Mi móvil es viejo; el tuyo es nuevo.","sol":"Mi móvil es viejo; el tuyo es nuevo. The first clause names móvil directly, using mi as a possessive adjective; the second clause does not repeat móvil, so the full possessive pronoun el tuyo is needed instead.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first clause names the phone directly, using a possessive adjective.","The second clause doesn't repeat 'phone' — which form fits when the noun isn't restated?","Two clauses joined by a semicolon."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Complete with aquel or ese as appropriate, giving a reason: 'Prefiero ___ camisa que llevas puesta ahora mismo.' (the shirt the listener is wearing right now)","a":"esa — the shirt is close to the listener (they are wearing it), so the middle-distance form fits, not aquella.","sol":"Prefiero esa camisa que llevas puesta ahora mismo. The shirt belongs to the person being spoken to and is close to them, so the middle distance ese/esa is correct, not aquella, which would describe something further from both speakers.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Whose shirt is it, and how close is it to the listener?","Which of the two distances fits something the listener is holding or wearing right now?","Give the form and a short reason about distance."],"codes":["Determiners"]},{"q":"Write two Spanish sentences about a family photo, each correctly using one possessive pronoun (el mío/la mía, el tuyo/la tuya, el suyo/la suya, el nuestro/la nuestra or el vuestro/la vuestra).","a":"Two sentences, e.g. 'Mi madre está a la izquierda y la tuya a la derecha.' / 'Mi hermano es más alto que el tuyo.'","sol":"Example: En la foto, mi madre está a la izquierda y la tuya está a la derecha. Mi hermano es más alto que el tuyo. Each sentence uses one possessive pronoun (la tuya agreeing with madre; el tuyo agreeing with hermano), standing alone with no noun repeated after it, with a consistent tú addressee.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Name the noun each possessive pronoun will stand in for before you write anything.","Check every possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned, not the owner.","Two linked sentences, not a list."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Lee: 'Aquella casa al final de la calle es la nuestra; la tuya es la que tiene el jardín grande, ¿verdad?' Answer in English: which house belongs to the speaker's family (ours), and how is the listener's house identified?","a":"The speaker's family's house (ours) is the one at the end of the street, further away; the listener's is the one with the big garden.","sol":"Aquella casa al final de la calle es la nuestra — that house (over there) at the end of the street is ours; la tuya es la que tiene el jardín grande — yours is the one that has the big garden. aquella marks the far distance; la nuestra and la tuya are possessive pronouns standing for casa; la que introduces the subject relative clause identifying the listener's house.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Find the phrase saying which house is 'ours', and where it is.","Find the phrase describing the listener's house.","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Determiners","Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My cousins write letters to each other every month; theirs are longer than mine.'","a":"Mis primos se escriben cartas cada mes; las suyas son más largas que las mías.","sol":"Mis primos se escriben cartas cada mes; las suyas son más largas que las mías. se escriben cartas is a reciprocal reflexive ('write to each other'); 'theirs' and 'mine' both stand for cartas (feminine plural), so both take the possessive pronoun agreeing with cartas: las suyas, las mías.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which reflexive pronoun and verb form express 'write to each other' for a plural subject?","What plural feminine noun are 'theirs' and 'mine' both standing in for?","Two clauses joined by a semicolon, with both possessive pronouns agreeing with that noun."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using relative pronouns: 'That is the town where I was born, and I was very happy when I lived there.'","a":"Ese es el pueblo donde nací, y era muy feliz cuando vivía allí.","sol":"Ese es el pueblo donde nací, y era muy feliz cuando vivía allí. donde introduces a relative clause of place; cuando introduces a temporal clause, both keeping their normal meaning as relative words.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which relative word means 'where', introducing the first clause?","Which word means 'when', introducing the second clause as an ordinary temporal clause?","Two linked clauses in one sentence."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using the emphatic form: 'I really love the mountains; that surprised my friend, who prefers the beach.'","a":"A mí me encantan las montañas; aquello sorprendió a mi amigo, que prefiere la playa.","sol":"A mí me encantan las montañas; aquello sorprendió a mi amigo, que prefiere la playa. A mí adds emphasis to me encantan; aquello is the neuter pronoun referring to the whole situation just described, not one specific noun.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Which extra phrase, before me, adds emphasis without changing the meaning?","Which neuter pronoun refers to the whole idea just mentioned, rather than one noun?","Two clauses joined by a semicolon."],"codes":["Pronouns"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the four forms of aquel (m/f, singular/plural).","a":"aquel, aquella, aquellos, aquellas"},{"q":"What does el mío mean, and what part of speech is it?","a":"'Mine' — a possessive pronoun, standing alone with no noun following."},{"q":"What does el que mean in 'El que estudia más saca mejores notas'?","a":"'The one who' / 'whoever' — a subject relative pronoun standing for the person who studies."},{"q":"What is the fused form of con + ti?","a":"contigo"}]},"cards":[{"front":"aquella montaña","back":"that mountain (over there, further than ese). Aquella montaña al fondo es muy alta. (That mountain in the background is very tall.)","code":"Determiners","open":false},{"front":"aquel edificio","back":"that building (over there). Aquel edificio es el ayuntamiento. (That building is the town hall.)","code":"Determiners","open":false},{"front":"el mío / la mía","back":"mine — possessive pronoun, stands alone. Este bolso no es tuyo; es el mío. (This bag isn't yours; it's mine.)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"el tuyo / el suyo","back":"yours / his-hers-theirs — possessive pronoun, agrees with the thing owned.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"el nuestro / el vuestro","back":"ours / yours (plural) — possessive pronoun, agrees with the thing owned.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"el que estudia más","back":"the one who studies most — headless subject relative. El que estudia más saca mejores notas. (The one who studies most gets the best marks.)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"lo que dice","back":"what he/she says — lo que is invariable, used for an idea rather than one noun.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"aquello","back":"that (neuter pronoun) — refers to a whole idea, not one noun. Aquello no me gustó nada. (I didn't like that at all.)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"conmigo / contigo","back":"with me / with you — fused forms of con + mí / con + ti. ¿Vienes conmigo? (Are you coming with me?)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"a mí me gusta","back":"I really like (emphatic) — a mí adds emphasis without changing the meaning of me gusta.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"nos vemos","back":"we see each other — reciprocal reflexive, plural subject only.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"puedo veros / os puedo ver","back":"I can see you (plural) — nos and os go before the conjugated verb, or attach to the infinitive; both are correct.","code":"Pronouns","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: los determinantes aquel","items":["aquel (m sing) - that (over there)","aquella (f sing) - that (over there)","aquellos (m pl) - those (over there)","aquellas (f pl) - those (over there)","al fondo - in the background, at the far end","allí / allá - over there","aquello - that (neuter, a whole idea)"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: los pronombres posesivos y relativos","items":["el mío / la mía / los míos / las mías - mine","el tuyo / la tuya / los tuyos / las tuyas - yours (informal singular)","el suyo / la suya / los suyos / las suyas - his / hers / yours (formal) / theirs","el nuestro / la nuestra / los nuestros / las nuestras - ours","el vuestro / la vuestra / los vuestros / las vuestras - yours (informal plural)","el que / la que / los que / las que - the one(s) who/that (headless subject relative)","el cual / la cual / los cuales / las cuales - who/which (formal; only after a stated noun)","lo que - what, that which","conmigo - with me","contigo - with you"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: pronombres de nivel superior","items":["Object pronoun with a modal + infinitive: before the conjugated verb or attached to the infinitive, both correct — Nos puede ayudar. / Puede ayudarnos. (He/She can help us.)","Reciprocal reflexive (plural subject only): Nos escribimos cada semana. (We write to each other every week.)","Possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned: Mi libro es nuevo; el tuyo es viejo. (My book is new; yours is old.)","Possessive adjective (noun stated) vs possessive pronoun (no noun): mi coche / el mío. Example: Mi coche es viejo, pero el tuyo es nuevo. (My car is old, but yours is new.)","el que como relativo sin antecedente ('el que' = quien); el cual solo después de un sustantivo. El que llega tarde no puede entrar. (Whoever arrives late cannot come in.) Mi vecina, la cual trabaja en el hospital, llega tarde. (My neighbour, who works at the hospital, arrives late.)","lo que for an unspecified idea: Lo que más me gusta es viajar. (What I like most is travelling.)","Pronoun after a preposition: para mí, con ella, a nosotros — except conmigo, contigo."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:15:11.705Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the grammar is sound throughout; fix the answer-revealing hint on the 'girl who lives next door' translation, the aquello/aquel placement in the why-section and vocab list, and the give-away title on the first worked example, and this is publishable."}}},"3.2.2.2":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.2.2.2","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Higher tier verb phrases add the grammar that lets you talk about hypothetical situations, wishes, ongoing actions and passive events — exactly the range Paper 4's higher-mark writing tasks and Paper 1/3's harder texts reward. Getting the subjunctive, the passive and the various time-related multi-verb structures right, rather than avoiding them, is often what separates the higher writing bands from the lower ones.","idea":[{"code":"Negation","h":"ya no, tampoco, ni…ni","t":"ya no means 'no longer': Ya no vivo en Madrid (I no longer live in Madrid). (no) tampoco means 'not … either' / 'neither': No me gusta el fútbol, y a mi hermano tampoco (I don't like football, and neither does my brother). (no) … ni … ni … means 'neither … nor', used for emphasis: No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero para viajar (I have neither time nor money to travel). All of these keep the normal Spanish double-negative pattern, with no before the verb when the negative word follows it."},{"code":"Inflectional morphology","h":"Trickier verb forms: plurals, spelling changes, the imperative and the present subjunctive","t":"The future, conditional and imperfect all need their plural forms too, not just singular: hablaremos/hablaréis/hablarán (future); hablaríamos/hablaríais/hablarían (conditional); the irregular imperfect verbs ir, ser and ver also have plural forms (íbamos, erais, veían), used for both a habitual 'used to' meaning and an ongoing 'was/were -ing' meaning. The future and conditional also share five irregular stems, now credit-bearing in the plural too: saber (sabr-), querer (querr-), venir (vendr-), decir (dir-), salir (saldr-) — sabremos, querría, vendréis, dirían, saldríamos. Some verbs change their spelling to keep the same sound, and this is now credit-bearing: sacar → saqué (c→qu before e), coger → cojo (present tense g→j), llegar → llegué (g→gu). Certain -ir verbs change their stem in the third person of the preterite: pedir → pidió/pidieron (e→i), dormir → durmió/durmieron (o→u). The imperative gives direct commands in the tú and vosotros forms: ¡Habla! (Speak!, tú), ¡Hablad! (Speak!, vosotros). The present subjunctive is required, for singular persons only, for five specific verbs — hacer, ser, ir, venir, tener — after cuando referring to the future (Cuando tenga tiempo, te llamaré — When I have time, I'll call you), after verbs of wishing, command, request or emotion (Quiero que vengas — I want you to come), after que in certain fixed patterns, and after para que to express purpose (Se lo explico para que tenga tiempo de prepararse — I'm explaining it to her so that she has time to prepare)."},{"code":"Multi-verb expressions","h":"acabar de, the passive, seguir/llevar + gerund, desde hace","t":"acabar de + infinitive means 'to have just done something': Acabo de llegar (I have just arrived). The passive voice is formed with ser + past participle + por: La casa fue construida por mi abuelo (The house was built by my grandfather); an alternative, more common passive uses se + a third-person verb: Se construyó la casa en 1950 (The house was built in 1950). seguir + present participle means 'to keep on doing': Sigo estudiando español (I keep on studying Spanish); llevar + a time period + present participle means 'to have been doing something for [time]': Llevo dos años estudiando español (I have been studying Spanish for two years) — the same idea as desde hace, but built differently: Estudio español desde hace dos años means the same thing."},{"code":"Impersonal verbs","h":"parece, basta, falta, hace falta, vale la pena","t":"These verbs describe a general situation rather than naming who is affected, though they can take an indirect object pronoun to show who: (me) parece que … ([it] seems to me that…); basta con + infinitive (it is enough to…); (me) falta / hace falta + noun or infinitive (I need/lack…, it's necessary to…); vale la pena + infinitive (it's worth…): No vale la pena discutir por esto (It's not worth arguing over this)."},{"code":"Adjectival phrases","h":"lo + adjective, possessives after the verb, and superlative adjectives","t":"lo + a masculine singular adjective turns it into an abstract noun: lo bueno es que… (the good thing is that…); lo más difícil (the hardest part). Possessive adjectives can stand as a complement after a verb like ser, agreeing with the thing owned, not written next to the noun: Este libro es mío (This book is mine — not 'el libro mío'); Estos boletos son nuestros (These tickets are ours). The superlative uses el/la/los/las + más/menos + adjective, with de to mean 'in/of': el estudiante más trabajador de la clase (the hardest-working student in the class); irregular superlatives include el mejor (the best) and el peor (the worst)."},{"code":"Adverbial phrases","h":"Superlative adverbs","t":"Superlative adverb structures compare an action against everyone or everything. The regular way is to extend the comparative: más/menos + adverb + que todos / que nadie: Corre más rápido que nadie (He runs faster than anyone — the fastest); Este equipo juega peor que todos los demás (This team plays worse than all the others — the worst). The irregular forms mejor and peor are used in the same way (juega mejor que nadie), and lo más … posible expresses 'as … as possible': Habla lo más despacio posible (Speak as slowly as possible)."},{"code":"Prepositions","h":"antes de / después de + infinitive, and prepositions in questions","t":"antes de and después de are followed by the infinitive, not a conjugated verb, even when the subject is the same as the main clause: Antes de salir, cierro la puerta con llave (Before leaving, I lock the door); Después de comer, vamos a dar un paseo (After eating, we're going to go for a walk). In questions, the preposition stays with its question word at the front, unlike informal English, which often moves it to the end: ¿Con quién hablas? (Who are you talking with? / With whom are you talking?); ¿De qué hablan? (What are they talking about?)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Negation with ya no and tampoco","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'I no longer eat meat, and my sister doesn't eat fish either.'","steps":["'I no longer eat meat' uses ya no before the verb: Ya no como carne.","'my sister' is the new subject of the second clause, so it must be stated: mi hermana — the verb will then be 3rd person singular.","'doesn't eat fish either' uses tampoco directly before the verb, which replaces no entirely (no is only needed if tampoco comes after the verb instead): tampoco come pescado.","Full sentence: Ya no como carne, y mi hermana tampoco come pescado."],"cues":["Which word means 'no longer', and where does it go relative to the verb?","Who is the subject of the second clause?","Which word means 'not … either', and where does it go relative to the verb?","Write the complete sentence."]},{"title":"The present subjunctive after cuando referring to the future","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'When I have more free time, I will learn to play the guitar.'","steps":["This cuando clause refers to a future, not-yet-true situation, so it needs the present subjunctive of tener: tenga.","'more free time' follows: más tiempo libre.","The result clause uses the ordinary future tense, not the subjunctive: aprenderé a tocar la guitarra.","Full sentence: Cuando tenga más tiempo libre, aprenderé a tocar la guitarra."],"cues":["Does cuando here refer to something already happening, or something not yet true?","What follows tenga to say 'more free time'?","Which tense does the main, result clause take — subjunctive too, or the ordinary future?","Write the complete sentence."]},{"title":"The passive with se, and acabar de","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'This bridge was built in 1920, and workers have just finished repairing it.'","steps":["'was built' uses the se-passive with a preterite verb: se construyó.","The bridge is the subject and the year gives the time frame: Este puente se construyó en 1920.","'have just finished repairing it' uses acabar de + infinitive: acaban de terminar de repararlo.","Full sentence: Este puente se construyó en 1920, y los obreros acaban de terminar de repararlo."],"cues":["'was built' does not name who built it — which passive structure fits that?","What is the subject of se construyó, and where does the year go?","Which structure means 'have just done' something?","Write the complete sentence."]},{"title":"Superlative adverb versus comparative","setup":"Translate into Spanish, choosing between a comparative and a superlative: 'Of all my friends, Ana studies the hardest; she studies harder than me too.'","steps":["'studies the hardest' of the whole group compares against everyone else: estudia más que nadie.","'Of all my friends, Ana' opens the sentence: De todos mis amigos, Ana.","'studies harder than me' compares just one other person: estudia más que yo.","Full sentence: De todos mis amigos, Ana estudia más que nadie; también estudia más que yo."],"cues":["Is 'studies the hardest' comparing Ana with one person, or with everyone?","How does the sentence open, naming the whole group?","What follows que when comparing against just one person?","Write both clauses."]}],"check":[{"q":"What does ya no mean, and where does it go relative to the verb?","a":"'No longer' — it goes directly before the verb."},{"q":"After cuando referring to something not yet true (a future condition), which mood is used?","a":"The present subjunctive."},{"q":"What is the difference between más rápido que su hermano and más rápido que nadie?","a":"que + one person/thing is an ordinary comparative ('faster than his brother'); que nadie extends the same comparative to everyone, giving a superlative meaning ('faster than anyone' = the fastest)."},{"q":"What form follows antes de and después de, even with the same subject as the main clause?","a":"The infinitive, not a conjugated verb."}]},"room":{"facts":["ya no = no longer; (no) tampoco = not … either; (no) ni … ni … = neither … nor.","cuando + present subjunctive refers to a future, not-yet-true situation; cuando + present indicative refers to a habitual or current fact.","Passive: ser + past participle + por (names who did it), or se + third-person verb (does not name who).","Adjectival superlative: el/la/los/las + más/menos + adjective + de (of/in a group). Adverbial superlative: más/menos + adverb + que todos / que nadie (juega mejor que nadie), or lo más … posible.","antes de / después de + infinitive, even when the subject doesn't change: Antes de salir, cierro la puerta."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I no longer play tennis.'","a":"Ya no juego al tenis.","sol":"Ya no juego al tenis. ya no goes directly before the conjugated verb.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which two words mean 'no longer'?","Where do they go relative to the verb?","The sentence is just three words: negative phrase, verb, object."],"codes":["Negation"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'It's worth trying.'","a":"Vale la pena intentarlo.","sol":"Vale la pena intentarlo. vale la pena is followed directly by an infinitive; lo (it) attaches to intentar as its object.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase means 'it's worth'?","What follows it — a conjugated verb or an infinitive?","Impersonal phrase, then the infinitive with its pronoun attached."],"codes":["Impersonal verbs"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'This car is mine.'","a":"Este coche es mío.","sol":"Este coche es mío. The possessive stands as a complement after ser, agreeing with coche (masculine), not attached to the noun.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Where does the possessive go here — next to the noun, or after the verb ser?","Which form of 'mine' agrees with a masculine noun?","Demonstrative + noun, ser, then the possessive."],"codes":["Adjectival phrases"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using tú commands: 'Speak more slowly and eat your vegetables!'","a":"¡Habla más despacio y come tus verduras!","sol":"¡Habla más despacio y come tus verduras! habla and come are the affirmative tú imperatives of hablar and comer, both regular, matching the 3rd person singular present form.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Which form of the verb gives a direct command to one friend?","Both verbs here are regular in that form.","Two commands joined by y, with exclamation marks."],"codes":["Inflectional morphology"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Before eating, we wash our hands.'","a":"Antes de comer, nos lavamos las manos.","sol":"Antes de comer, nos lavamos las manos. antes de is followed by the infinitive comer, even though the subject (nosotros) is the same in both parts.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["What form does the verb take after antes de, even with the same subject?","'We wash our hands' is a reflexive action — check the verb form carries the right pronoun.","Time clause first, then the reflexive verb and its object."],"codes":["Prepositions"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'When you have time, would you like to help me?' (informal singular 'you', referring to a future possibility)","a":"Cuando tengas tiempo, ¿querrías ayudarme?","sol":"Cuando tengas tiempo, ¿querrías ayudarme? cuando refers to a future, not-yet-true moment, so tener takes the present subjunctive (tengas); querrías is the conditional of querer, one of the five irregular conditional stems, for a polite request.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Does cuando here describe something already true, or something that hasn't happened yet?","Which mood does tener take in that case?","Write the complete sentence, keeping the request polite rather than a blunt command."],"codes":["Inflectional morphology"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I have been living in this town for six years.'","a":"Vivo en este pueblo desde hace seis años.","sol":"Vivo en este pueblo desde hace seis años. Present tense (vivo) with desde hace + a time period expresses an action continuing up to now — the same idea as Llevo seis años viviendo en este pueblo, built with llevar + gerund instead.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Which tense does the verb take with desde hace, present or past?","Where does desde hace go in the sentence?","Verb in the present, then the place, then desde hace + time."],"codes":["Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'The best thing about this school is the teachers, but the hardest thing is the exams.'","a":"Lo mejor de este colegio son los profesores, pero lo más difícil son los exámenes.","sol":"Lo mejor de este colegio son los profesores, pero lo más difícil son los exámenes. lo + adjective (lo mejor, lo más difícil) turns the adjective into an abstract noun; son agrees with the plural noun that follows (profesores, exámenes), not with lo.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which structure turns an adjective into 'the [adjective] thing'?","Does the verb that follows agree with that structure itself, or with the noun that comes after it?","Two clauses joined by pero, each starting with lo + adjective."],"codes":["Adjectival phrases"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I have neither the time nor the money to go on holiday this year.'","a":"No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero para irme de vacaciones este año.","sol":"No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero para irme de vacaciones este año. no … ni … ni … gives 'neither … nor', and the article is normally dropped after ni; para + infinitive expresses purpose.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which structure gives 'neither … nor', and does it need no as well before the verb?","What links 'the money' to 'to go on holiday' — which small word means 'to/in order to'?","No + tener, ni X ni Y, then para + infinitive."],"codes":["Negation"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'This bridge was designed by a famous architect, and it has just been repaired.'","a":"Este puente fue diseñado por un arquitecto famoso, y acaba de ser reparado.","sol":"Este puente fue diseñado por un arquitecto famoso, y acaba de ser reparado. fue diseñado por names who did the action, so it uses ser + past participle + por, not the se-passive; acaba de ser reparado combines acabar de + infinitive with a second passive.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Since 'by a famous architect' is stated, which passive structure fits — se, or ser + participle + por?","'has just been repaired' needs a structure for 'have just done' something, followed by a second passive.","Write the complete sentence with both passive structures."],"codes":["Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Marta runs fastest of all the runners, but she says it isn't worth competing if you don't enjoy it.'","a":"Marta corre más rápido que todos los corredores, pero dice que no vale la pena competir si no lo disfrutas.","sol":"Marta corre más rápido que todos los corredores, pero dice que no vale la pena competir si no lo disfrutas. 'Runs fastest of all the runners' is expressed by comparing against the whole group with que todos los corredores (que nadie would also be accepted); no vale la pena + infinitive is the impersonal 'it isn't worth'.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["How do you compare Marta against the whole group of runners using the ordinary comparative?","Which impersonal phrase means 'it isn't worth'?","Write the complete sentence with both structures."],"codes":["Adverbial phrases","Impersonal verbs"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish about how your habits or your town have changed, using at least one Higher tier structure from each of these three ideas: Negation, Inflectional morphology (the subjunctive or a spelling-change verb), and Multi-verb expressions.","a":"A paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish, correctly using at least one structure from each of the three named ideas, describing genuine change over time.","sol":"Antes comía mucha comida basura, pero ya no como tanto porque quiero cuidarme mejor. El año pasado empecé a hacer ejercicio, y llevo tres meses yendo al gimnasio dos veces por semana. También ya no bebo tantos refrescos, y sigo intentando comer más verduras cada semana. Cuando tenga más tiempo el próximo verano, iré al gimnasio todos los días, porque creo que vale la pena. Mi pueblo también ha cambiado: se construyeron varios parques nuevos, y espero que mi pueblo tenga más parques en el futuro. (86 words: ya no appears twice for negation; empecé is a credit-bearing preterite spelling change (z→c), and cuando tenga / espero que ... tenga are genuine present subjunctive of tener, both for inflectional morphology; llevo... yendo and sigo intentando are multi-verb expressions.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Choose one clear example of each of the three required ideas before you start writing.","Describe genuine change: something you used to do, something you do now, and how a place has changed.","Write the paragraph, then check it against your three chosen structures and count the words."],"codes":["Negation","Inflectional morphology","Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Ya no vivo en Madrid; acabo de mudarme a Sevilla, aunque sigo trabajando para la misma empresa.'","a":"I no longer live in Madrid; I have just moved to Seville, although I keep on working for the same company.","sol":"Ya no vivo en Madrid; acabo de mudarme a Sevilla, aunque sigo trabajando para la misma empresa — I no longer live in Madrid; I have just moved to Seville, although I keep on working for the same company. ya no = no longer; acabo de + infinitive = have just; sigo + gerund = keep on.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["ya no changes the meaning of the first verb — how?","acabar de and seguir + gerund each carry a time meaning.","Three linked clauses in one English sentence."],"codes":["Negation","Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Lee: 'El puente fue construido por mi abuelo, y cuando tenga dinero lo voy a reparar, porque vale la pena conservarlo.' Answer in English: who built the bridge, and when does the writer plan to repair it?","a":"The writer's grandfather built it; the writer will repair it when they have money.","sol":"fue construido por mi abuelo — it was built by my grandfather (passive with ser + participle + por); cuando tenga dinero lo voy a reparar — when I have money I'm going to repair it (cuando + present subjunctive for a future event); vale la pena conservarlo — it's worth preserving it.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the passive phrase naming who did the building.","Find the cuando clause giving the condition for repairing it.","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Multi-verb expressions","Inflectional morphology","Impersonal verbs"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'not … either'.","a":"tampoco"},{"q":"Translate: 'lo mejor de la fiesta'.","a":"the best thing about the party"},{"q":"How is the se-passive formed, instead of ser + past participle?","a":"se + a third-person verb (se construyó)."},{"q":"What does acabar de + infinitive mean?","a":"To have just done something."}]},"cards":[{"front":"ya no","back":"no longer. Ya no vivo aquí. (I no longer live here.)","code":"Negation","open":false},{"front":"(no) tampoco","back":"not ... either, neither. A mí tampoco me gusta. (I don't like it either.)","code":"Negation","open":false},{"front":"cuando + subjuntivo (futuro)","back":"when [not yet true] + subjunctive. Cuando tenga tiempo, te llamaré. (When I have time, I'll call you.)","code":"Inflectional morphology","open":false},{"front":"saqué, llegué, cojo","back":"credit-bearing spelling changes (sacar c→qu, llegar g→gu, coger present g→j). Ayer saqué fotos del castillo. (Yesterday I took photos of the castle.)","code":"Inflectional morphology","open":false},{"front":"pidió, durmió","back":"credit-bearing preterite stem changes in -ir verbs (pedir e→i, dormir o→u). El camarero le pidió el pasaporte. (The waiter asked him for his passport.)","code":"Inflectional morphology","open":false},{"front":"acabar de + infinitivo","back":"to have just done something. Acabo de llegar. (I have just arrived.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"ser + participio + por","back":"passive, naming who did it. Fue construido por mi abuelo. (It was built by my grandfather.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"se + verbo (pasiva)","back":"passive, without naming who. Se construyó en 1950. (It was built in 1950.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"llevar + tiempo + gerundio","back":"to have been doing something for [time]. Llevo dos años estudiando español. (I've been studying Spanish for two years.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"vale la pena + infinitivo","back":"it's worth doing. No vale la pena discutir. (It's not worth arguing.)","code":"Impersonal verbs","open":false},{"front":"hace falta / falta + infinitivo o sustantivo","back":"it's necessary to / ... is needed. Hace falta estudiar más. (It's necessary to study more.)","code":"Impersonal verbs","open":false},{"front":"lo + adjetivo","back":"the [adjective] thing. Lo bueno es que tenemos tiempo. (The good thing is we have time.)","code":"Adjectival phrases","open":false},{"front":"más rápido / peor + que nadie","back":"faster / worse than anyone — a superlative meaning. Corre más rápido que nadie. (He runs faster than anyone.)","code":"Adverbial phrases","open":false},{"front":"antes de / después de + infinitivo","back":"before / after doing something. Antes de salir, cierro la puerta. (Before leaving, I lock the door.)","code":"Prepositions","open":false},{"front":"¿Con quién...? / ¿De qué...?","back":"With whom...? / About what...? — preposition stays with the question word. ¿Con quién hablas? (Who are you talking with?)","code":"Prepositions","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: negación y verbos impersonales","items":["ya no - no longer","tampoco - not... either, neither","ni... ni... - neither... nor","parece que - it seems that","basta con - it's enough to","hace falta / falta - it's necessary / ... is needed","vale la pena - it's worth it"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: estructuras de tiempo, comparativos y palabras clave","items":["acabar de + infinitivo - to have just done","seguir + gerundio - to keep on doing","llevar + tiempo + gerundio - to have been doing for [time]","desde hace + tiempo - for [time], continuing to now","antes de + infinitivo - before doing","después de + infinitivo - after doing","el mejor / el peor - the best / the worst","el arquitecto - the architect","el puente - the bridge"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: subjuntivo, pasiva y superlativos","items":["subjuntivo presente (hacer, ser, ir, venir, tener) tras cuando (futuro), deseo/mandato/emoción, y para que. Example: Cuando tenga tiempo, te llamaré. (When I have time, I'll call you.)","imperativo afirmativo (tú, vosotros), irregular en algunos verbos. Example: ¡Ven aquí y ten cuidado! (Come here and be careful!)","pasiva con ser + participio + por (con agente) o se + verbo (sin agente). Example: Este puente fue construido por mi abuelo. (This bridge was built by my grandfather.)","posesivos después del verbo (complemento). Example: Este libro es mío, pero esos boletos son nuestros. (This book is mine, but those tickets are ours.)","superlativo: el/la/los/las + más/menos + adjetivo + de. Comparativo: más/menos + adjetivo + que. Example: Es la estudiante más trabajadora de la clase. (She is the hardest-working student in the class.)","superlativo adverbial: más/menos (o mejor/peor) + adverbio + que nadie/que todos. Example: Corre más rápido que nadie. (He runs faster than anyone — the fastest.)","cambios ortográficos, con crédito a partir de Higher tier: sacar → saqué; llegar → llegué; coger → cojo. Example: Ayer saqué fotos y llegué tarde a casa. (Yesterday I took photos and got home late.)","cambios en la raíz del pretérito (-ir): pedir → pidió/pidieron; dormir → durmió/durmieron. Example: El niño pidió agua y luego durmió toda la noche. (The child asked for water and then slept all night.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:15:11.706Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key checks out and the translations are sound Spanish, but the model paragraph awards itself a z→c spelling change (empecé) that the lesson and grammar sheet never teach, and the adverbial-superlative example uses más alone for 'the hardest', so fix those two before publishing."}}},"Foundation tier vocabulary":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"Foundation tier vocabulary","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Appendix 2 is the word list every Foundation tier reading, listening, writing and speaking task is built from, and two columns printed beside each headword tell you exactly what you are responsible for: the Tier column shows which items you need, and the Selection-principle column shows how each one was chosen. Knowing how to read the columns tells you exactly which words you are responsible for, and knowing the words themselves earns marks everywhere else — so this room covers both the coding system and real use of a representative sample of the words.","idea":[{"code":"Tier F","h":"The Tier column: F and F/H","t":"Headwords in Appendix 2 carry the code F to mean they belong to the Foundation tier list. A smaller set — mainly the 30 multi-word phrases (such as me gustaría, tengo que, hace buen tiempo) and 20 geographical or cultural items (such as España) — carries the code F/H instead, meaning that item is required at both Foundation and Higher tier: it must be known productively (speaking and writing) and receptively (listening and reading) at Foundation tier, and it carries forward unchanged into the Higher tier list rather than being replaced by something else. The specification does not state that a plain F headword is excluded from Higher tier study — F simply marks it as a Foundation tier item; F/H is the code that explicitly flags an item as shared between the two tiers."},{"code":"Selection principle","h":"The Selection-principle column: R, O, O>, C","t":"Each headword also carries a Selection-principle code, explained in the key printed under the vocabulary table. R (Required) marks a word that is part of the DfE's Annex E list of grammar-linked words. O (Optional) marks a word drawn from within the top 2000 most frequent words in Spanish that is not on the Annex E list. O> (Optional) marks a word drawn from outside the top 2000 most frequent words — that is the whole of the definition given, so as far as the key is concerned a word counts as O> once it falls outside that frequency band, however specialised it is. C marks the culturally or geographically specific items — festival names, place names and similar — which are listed regardless of how frequent they are, because they are included for what they refer to rather than for their frequency."}],"examples":[{"title":"Identifying an F/H item and using it in a sentence","setup":"The appendix lists 'me gustaría' with the code F/H. Explain what this code means for a Foundation tier candidate, and write one Spanish sentence using me gustaría to say 'I would like to visit Spain'.","steps":["F/H means this phrase is required at both Foundation and Higher tier, so a Foundation candidate must already know it productively and receptively, and it carries forward unchanged into Higher tier study.","me gustaría is followed directly by an infinitive to say what you would like to do: me gustaría visitar.","Add the place: visitar España.","Put together: F/H means it is required at both tiers; the sentence is Me gustaría visitar España."],"cues":["What does the code F/H tell you about who must know this phrase?","What form of the verb follows me gustaría?","Which country are you saying you would like to visit?","Give both parts of the answer: the meaning of the code, and the full sentence."]},{"title":"Reading comprehension: a reason clause with porque","setup":"Translate into English: 'Mis padres van a la playa porque hace buen tiempo.'","steps":["Mis padres van a la playa translates as 'My parents go/are going to the beach'.","porque introduces the reason and translates simply as 'because'.","hace buen tiempo is a weather phrase meaning 'the weather is good' or 'it's nice weather'.","Full English answer: My parents are going to the beach because the weather is good."],"cues":["Who is doing the action, and where are they going?","What English word does porque translate to?","What does hace buen tiempo describe?","Write the complete English sentence."]},{"title":"Translating into Spanish: a hobby with jugar al","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'I play chess every Sunday.'","steps":["'I play' uses jugar, which changes its stem to jueg- in the yo form: juego.","'chess' follows jugar al, since ajedrez is masculine: juego al ajedrez.","'every Sunday' is todos los domingos, added at the end.","Put together: Juego al ajedrez todos los domingos."],"cues":["What is the yo form of jugar, with its stem change?","Which preposition and article combine before ajedrez?","How do you say 'every Sunday'?","Write the complete Spanish sentence."]},{"title":"Using a C-coded cultural item in a sentence","setup":"The appendix lists 'España' with the code C. Explain what the C code means here, and write one Spanish sentence saying 'Spain is a beautiful country.'","steps":["C marks España as a geographically specific item, listed because of what it refers to rather than because of how frequent the word is.","'Spain is' translates as España es.","'a beautiful country' is un país precioso, with país masculine so the article and adjective agree.","Put together: C means it is a geographical item listed regardless of frequency; the sentence is España es un país precioso."],"cues":["What kind of item does the code C mark — is it about frequency, or about subject matter?","How do you say 'Spain is' in Spanish?","What gender is país, and how does that affect the article and adjective?","Give both parts of the answer: the meaning of the code, and the full sentence."]}],"check":[{"q":"What does the Tier code F/H mean for a headword in Appendix 2?","a":"It is required at both Foundation and Higher tier, known productively and receptively, and carries forward unchanged into Higher tier."},{"q":"What is the difference between R and O in the Selection-principle column?","a":"R marks a word on the DfE's Annex E grammar-linked list; O marks a word within the top 2000 most frequent words that is not on that list."},{"q":"Does the specification set any further test for an O>-coded word beyond being outside the top 2000 most frequent words?","a":"No — any word chosen for the topic that falls outside the top 2000 most frequent words counts as O>."},{"q":"Translate: 'hace buen tiempo'.","a":"The weather is good / it's nice weather."}]},"room":{"facts":["Tier column: F = Foundation tier; F/H = required at both Foundation and Higher tier, carried forward unchanged.","Selection-principle column: R = on the DfE's Annex E grammar-linked list; O = optional, within the top 2000 most frequent words; O> = optional, outside the top 2000 most frequent words; C = cultural/geographical item, listed regardless of frequency.","me gustaría + infinitive = I would like to; tengo que + infinitive = I have to; hace buen/mal tiempo = the weather is good/bad.","jugar changes its stem to jueg- except in the nosotros/vosotros forms, and is followed by al before a masculine activity: jugar al fútbol, jugar al ajedrez.","porque (because, mid-sentence) introduces a reason; the same idea to open a sentence is como."],"questions":[{"q":"What does the Tier code F mean for a headword in Appendix 2?","a":"It is a Foundation tier headword.","sol":"F marks a word as belonging to the Foundation tier list — the level of vocabulary every Foundation candidate is expected to know.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is the more common of the two Tier codes.","Which single tier does this code name on its own?","One short phrase."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Which Selection-principle code marks a word chosen for its cultural or geographical content rather than for how frequent it is?","a":"C","sol":"C marks the culturally or geographically specific items — such as festival and place names — included regardless of frequency, unlike R, O and O>, which are all frequency-related.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This code stands apart from the three frequency-based codes.","Think of a festival name or place name in the appendix.","One letter."],"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Me gustaría ir a la playa este fin de semana.'","a":"I would like to go to the beach this weekend.","sol":"I would like to go to the beach this weekend. me gustaría + infinitive (ir) gives 'I would like to go'; este fin de semana is 'this weekend'.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["me gustaría is followed by an infinitive — what does that combination mean?","Where does the sentence say this person would like to go?","Give the complete English sentence, including when."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I have to study on Saturdays.'","a":"Tengo que estudiar los sábados.","sol":"Tengo que estudiar los sábados. tengo que + infinitive gives 'I have to'; 'on Saturdays' (a repeated day) is los sábados, no separate word for 'on'.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which fixed phrase means 'I have to'?","What follows the 'have to' phrase — an infinitive or a conjugated verb?","One complete sentence."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"An appendix entry is marked 'O'. Explain what this tells you, and give one difference between O and O>.","a":"O means the word is optional and falls within the top 2000 most frequent Spanish words; O> is also optional but falls outside the top 2000.","sol":"O marks an optional word (not on the Annex E required list) that is still within the top 2000 most frequent Spanish words; the difference from O> is purely this frequency band — O> is optional but rarer, falling outside the top 2000.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Is an O-coded word required, or optional?","Compare O directly with O> — what single thing changes between them?","Give two things: what O means, and the one difference from O>."],"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Vivo en un pueblo pequeño cerca de la costa. Me gusta porque hay una playa bonita y el ambiente es tranquilo.' What does the writer like about where they live, and why?","a":"They like their village: it has a pretty beach and a calm/peaceful atmosphere.","sol":"The writer likes their village (Me gusta) because there is a pretty beach (hay una playa bonita) and the atmosphere is calm/peaceful (el ambiente es tranquilo) — three points: what they like, and the two reasons joined after porque.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the reason word porque in the second sentence.","What comes after porque?","Answer in English, saying what they like and why."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My parents watch television every evening, but I prefer to play football.'","a":"Mis padres ven la televisión todas las noches, pero yo prefiero jugar al fútbol.","sol":"Mis padres ven la televisión todas las noches, pero yo prefiero jugar al fútbol. ver is irregular in the ellos form (ven); prefiero is a stem-changing verb (e→ie) followed by the infinitive jugar, which itself takes al before fútbol. 'Every evening' may be todas las tardes or todas las noches; both are accepted.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This verb is irregular for 'they' — check its form before writing.","The verb for 'prefer' is followed directly by another verb — in which form?","One sentence with two linked clauses."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"An entry is marked with the Selection-principle code R. What does this tell you about how it was chosen, and how does that differ from an O-coded entry?","a":"R means the word is part of the DfE's Annex E list of grammar-linked words; O means the word is within the top 2000 most frequent words but not on that Annex E list.","sol":"R and O rest on different grounds: R marks a word as belonging to the DfE's Annex E list of grammar-linked words, while O marks a word chosen purely for being within the top 2000 most frequent words, without being on that Annex E list.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["R is tied to a specific named list, not just frequency.","Which list is that, and what kind of words does it contain?","Explain both codes and how they differ."],"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Todos los años vamos a España para visitar a mis abuelos, y siempre hace mucho calor en verano.'","a":"Every year we go to Spain to visit my grandparents, and it's always very hot in summer.","sol":"Every year we go to Spain to visit my grandparents, and it's always very hot in summer. vamos a España is 'we go to Spain' (ir a + place); para visitar is a separate purpose clause, 'in order to visit'; hace mucho calor is a weather phrase meaning 'it is very hot'.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Who is 'we' going to visit, and where?","What season is named at the end, and what weather phrase goes with it?","Give the complete English sentence."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Write five short sentences in Spanish about your free time, each correctly using one of these phrases: me gustaría, tengo que, hace buen tiempo, jugar al + a sport, and quiero + infinitive.","a":"Five sentences, e.g. Me gustaría aprender a nadar. / Tengo que hacer los deberes. / Cuando hace buen tiempo, voy al parque. / Juego al baloncesto los sábados. / Quiero ver una película.","sol":"Example: Me gustaría aprender a nadar este verano. Tengo que hacer los deberes antes de salir. Cuando hace buen tiempo, voy al parque con mis amigos. Los fines de semana juego al baloncesto. Quiero ver una película esta noche. Each sentence uses one target phrase with correct grammar: me gustaría + infinitive, tengo que + infinitive, hace buen tiempo as a time clause, jugar al + a masculine sport, and quiero + infinitive.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Which of the phrases need an infinitive straight after them?","Which phrase describes the weather, and how can it start a sentence?","Five separate, complete sentences."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Write a short paragraph of approximately 50 words in Spanish about your last holiday, using at least two Foundation tier phrases from this room (for example fui a, me gustó, hacía buen tiempo, jugué al).","a":"About 50 words in the past tense using at least two of: fui a, me gustó, hacía buen tiempo, jugué al.","sol":"El verano pasado fui a España con mi familia. Hacía muy buen tiempo, así que fuimos a la playa casi todos los días. Me gustó mucho porque el mar estaba muy limpio. También jugué al voleibol con mi hermano y comimos paella en un restaurante cerca del hotel. (48 words: fui a, hacía buen tiempo, me gustó and jugué al all appear.)","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Choose at least two phrases from the list before you start, and plan one sentence around each.","Keep every verb in the past tense, since this describes a holiday that has already happened.","Write the paragraph, then count the words and check it is close to 50."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"An appendix entry reads: 'la enología — O>'. A student says this word is too specialised to really be O>-coded. Using only the specification's own definition of O>, say whether the student is right.","a":"No — the specification's only test for O> is that the word is optional and falls outside the top 2000 most frequent words; it sets no further limit on how specialised a word may be, so la enología can validly be O> if it meets that one condition.","sol":"The student is not right: the specification defines O> only as 'optional, drawn from outside the top 2000 most frequent words'. It sets no further limit on how specialised a word may be within that band, so a specialised word like la enología (oenology, the study of wine) is a valid O> entry provided it genuinely falls outside the top 2000.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Go back to exactly how the specification defines O> — read it carefully.","Separate what the code technically requires from a judgement about whether the word is a sensible choice to include.","State clearly whether the student's objection is supported by the specification's own wording."],"codes":["Selection principle"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which Tier code marks a headword as belonging to the Foundation tier list?","a":"F"},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'I have to' followed by an infinitive.","a":"Tengo que"},{"q":"A word is coded O rather than O>. Is it more or less frequent than an O>-coded word?","a":"More frequent — O falls within the top 2000 most frequent words, O> falls outside it."},{"q":"Translate: 'jugar al baloncesto'.","a":"to play basketball"}]},"cards":[{"front":"me gustaría + infinitivo","back":"I would like to. Me gustaría visitar España. (I would like to visit Spain.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"tengo que + infinitivo","back":"I have to. Tengo que estudiar. (I have to study.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"hace buen/mal tiempo","back":"the weather is good/bad. Hace buen tiempo hoy. (The weather is good today.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"jugar al + deporte","back":"to play + sport (stem-changing, jueg-). Juego al ajedrez. (I play chess.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"la playa","back":"the beach. Vamos a la playa. (We're going to the beach.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"el fin de semana","back":"the weekend. Este fin de semana voy al parque. (This weekend I'm going to the park.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"los abuelos","back":"grandparents. Visito a mis abuelos. (I visit my grandparents.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"porque","back":"because. Me gusta porque es divertido. (I like it because it's fun.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"España","back":"Spain. España es un país precioso. (Spain is a beautiful country.)","code":"Selection principle","open":false},{"front":"el fútbol","back":"football. Juego al fútbol los sábados. (I play football on Saturdays.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"mucho / mucha / muchos / muchas","back":"a lot of, much, many (agrees with the noun). Hace mucho calor. (It's very hot.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"quiero + infinitivo","back":"I want to. Quiero aprender español. (I want to learn Spanish.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"voy a + infinitivo","back":"I am going to. Voy a jugar al tenis. (I am going to play tennis.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"prefiero + infinitivo","back":"I prefer to. Prefiero jugar al fútbol. (I prefer to play football.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"todos los días","back":"every day. Juego al fútbol todos los días. (I play football every day.)","code":"Tier F","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario Foundation tier: frases F/H más usadas","items":["me gustaría + infinitivo - I would like to","tengo que + infinitivo - I have to","hace buen tiempo - the weather is good","hace mal tiempo - the weather is bad","jugar al fútbol - to play football","jugar al ajedrez - to play chess","el fin de semana - the weekend","todos los días - every day","el año pasado - last year"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario Foundation tier: lugares y familia (F y C)","items":["la playa - the beach","el pueblo - the village, small town","la costa - the coast","el ambiente - the atmosphere","los abuelos - the grandparents","los padres - the parents","el hermano / la hermana - brother / sister","España - Spain","el mar - the sea","el restaurante - the restaurant"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: estructuras F/H frecuentes","items":["me gustaría + infinitivo. Example: Me gustaría visitar España. (I would like to visit Spain.)","tengo que + infinitivo. Example: Tengo que estudiar los sábados. (I have to study on Saturdays.)","jugar al + deporte, con cambio de raíz (jueg-) menos en nosotros/vosotros. Example: Juego al ajedrez, pero mi hermano juega al fútbol. (I play chess, but my brother plays football.)","hace buen/mal tiempo (expresión del tiempo). Example: Hace buen tiempo hoy, así que vamos a la playa. (The weather is good today, so we're going to the beach.)","el pretérito para acciones pasadas y terminadas. Example: El verano pasado fui a España y jugué al voleibol. (Last summer I went to Spain and played volleyball.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:15:11.707Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly (translations, comprehension and the coding questions all match the specification map), so the kit is close to publishable; fix the 'todas las noches' key, re-code or replace the España card, and trim the two inferences added to the specification's definitions of F and O."}}}} };
